            <ArticleTitle>Coronary artery-left ventricle fistula complicating balloon angioplasty--a case report.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>879-83</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>The authors describe a coronary artery fistula complicated balloon angioplasty. The proximal left anterior descending coronary artery was dilated, but a septal branch was occluded by thrombus. Angioplasty was used on the septal branch, but a pseudoaneurysm communicating with the left ventricle occurred. Follow-up angiography revealed spontaneous closure of the fistula.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
